Model routing for new swarm workers:

Roles and cost
- GPT 6.1 Sol is the main chat and coordinator, with chat effort high. The saved worker default and normal implementation route is openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-terra with effort low.
- Corporate Copilot has no per-call cost for this user. Prefer it over paid workers whenever the very-simple-task rule below is satisfied, not only when paid routes fail. The user's observed trade-off is that Terra low costs less than Sol low and handles nontrivial work better than Copilot. These are local routing assumptions, not a universal model ranking or public-price claim. Corporate Copilot's public backend equivalence is unverified.
- For paid swarm workers outside adversarial review always explicitly use effort "low". The main chat's high setting is separate.
- For routine independent review spawn a fresh worker with label "routine-reviewer", model "openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-sol" and effort "low". For adversarial review use a fresh "adversarial-reviewer" with the same model and effort "xhigh". Choose the tier by the risk rules below. A reviewer must be independent of the author; reusing a worker can retain the wrong context, model or effort.
- The old label "reviewer" remains a Sol/xhigh compatibility alias for sessions with cached instructions. New delegations use the explicit review-tier labels.

Choose by uncertainty and risk
- Before spawning, name the task's uncertainty and choose the first matching route below. File count alone is not a reason to escalate. Split larger work into testable slices.
- Corporate Copilot: use model "x5-copilot:x5-airun-code-large" and effort "max" only for very simple local mechanical work. The steps, expected result and cheap deterministic check must already be specified. Examples: replace a literal in named files, make a small repetitive edit, add tests from fully written input/output cases, or collect references to an exact symbol. Algorithm selection, new branching/error handling, test-case design, bug diagnosis and cross-module reasoning go to Terra or Sol, even with a clear acceptance criterion. A small diff alone does not make a task simple.
- Sol: use model "openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-sol" and effort "low" for a bounded analysis or implementation whose unresolved ambiguity determines behavior, architecture or cross-module invariants, or after a focused Terra repair fails acceptance. Examples: reconcile conflicting requirements, choose a cross-module contract, or diagnose a failure spanning multiple subsystems. The coordinator resolves product decisions with the user rather than asking a worker to invent them.
- Terra: use model "openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-terra" and effort "low" for the remaining implementation, test design, reproduced-bug diagnosis and bounded refactoring tasks. It is the normal worker, not a last-resort experiment. Established multi-file contracts stay with Terra unless the unresolved-invariant rule above applies.
- If Copilot is unavailable, use Terra low. If Terra is unavailable, use Sol low. Report the replacement and respect project/provider permissions. For failed acceptance, diagnose the cause and allow one focused repair at the current tier, then hand unresolved context from Copilot to Terra low, or from Terra to Sol low. A Sol failure returns to the coordinator for decomposition or clarification, not repeated model cycling or higher paid-worker effort.
- Risk determines review depth independently of the author model. Sensitive work needs explicit positive and negative acceptance checks and adversarial review even when Terra or Copilot implements it. A green unit test alone is not production acceptance.

Choose review by risk
- Routine review is for bounded, understood, low-risk changes with observable acceptance checks, such as local presentation or mechanical refactors that preserve known contracts. A short diff alone does not establish low risk.
- Adversarial review is required for authorization, tenant/replay guards, sensitive data, salary semantics, durable serialization or migrations, concurrency, process/signal ownership, terminal escape sanitization, and risky final integration. Uncertain invariants or insufficient evidence also require adversarial review, even for a one-line change.
- Before delegation name the tier and why, bind the reviewed diff or commit and acceptance evidence. A routine reviewer must report newly discovered risk and request escalation, not approve a risky change on the cheap tier. Escalation spawns a fresh adversarial-reviewer.
- X5/max with label "review-precheck" may check a concrete mechanical checklist or test coverage. This is preliminary evidence, not the independent acceptance review; its author cannot be its only verifier. Risky changes still need independently authored positive/negative tests and adversarial Sol review.

Delegate with a contract
Provide the goal, expected behavior, owned paths and interfaces, invariants, forbidden side effects, a reproduction or reference, acceptance commands and edge cases. Distinguish new-object behavior from legacy roundtrip compatibility. One writer per worktree, isolate parallel writers.

Verify and escalate
Read each diff and independently check acceptance evidence before integration. Preserve tests and negative controls; a worker report is not verification. Allow one focused repair with a clear local diagnosis, then escalate missing design or repeated failures. Re-review when reviewed behavior or evidence changes, not automatically after unrelated edits. Report externally blocked checks.

Routing mechanics
Pass both explicit model and effort on each fresh spawn or spawn-capable assignment. The local pre-tool gate rejects missing model or effort when active; direct spawns labeled routine-reviewer require Sol/low, adversarial-reviewer and legacy reviewer require Sol/xhigh. It checks routes, not semantic risk, independence or freshness; other labels and internal dispatch do not receive tier enforcement. Jcode hooks fail open on errors and can be disabled. A reused worker keeps its model and effort regardless of assignment parameters. Use a fresh worker when the model or effort matters, without disrupting existing work. An explicit model wins over defaults; model "inherit" selects the coordinator and is not appropriate for normal routing. Cost claims should use observed route usage, retries and completed acceptance, not token counts treated as a public API invoice.

Privacy and structure
Keep endpoints, keys, private routing hosts, benchmark copies and results outside tracked repositories and public logs. Follow project disclosure rules. Always pass a label when spawning. In normal/light mode only the root spawns workers. This policy governs future delegation and does not authorize restarting sessions, reading credentials or contacting production services.
